Third-party teaching materials
==============================

Verified 13 October 2026 by fetching the SERC pages below. This file records
those pages and their stated reuse terms. It does not relicense them.

Original exercise text, images, and documentation in this repository are
CC BY 4.0 (LICENSE-CONTENT). Application code is MIT (LICENSE). Andrew
Laskowski confirmed that split. It does not apply to the University of Leeds
files or the Visible Geology SERC activity files. Those files state Creative
Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike terms, which are stricter than
CC BY 4.0 (non-commercial, and derivatives stay under the same license).

University of Leeds virtual field material
------------------------------------------

Jacqueline Houghton, University of Leeds. Virtual Landscapes Geological
Mapping. The landscapes are described as available at
www.see.leeds.ac.uk/virtual-landscapes/.

SERC activity pages, both fetched and confirmed as the same activity:

- https://serc.carleton.edu/teachearth/activities/197181.html
- https://serc.carleton.edu/NAGTWorkshops/online_field/activities/197181.html

SERC states that the accompanying field slips and notes on the Teach the Earth
page are offered under CC BY-NC-SA 3.0:
http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/

One file on that page, "04_Rhoscolyn whole area with outcrops.pdf", has no
reuse terms recorded. SERC says to contact the original creator for uses that
exceed fair use. Do not treat that file as CC BY-NC-SA or as CC BY.

The same SERC page lists the field-slip filenames used in this course,
including the topographic sheet, dip and strike sheets, Lighthouse Bay,
Rhoscolyn, and Three River Hills.

Related exercises that use those landscapes, also fetched:

- Mark Helper, The University of Texas at Austin. Geological Mapping of a
  Virtual Landscape (Lighthouse Bay), based on the Leeds landscape.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/NAGTWorkshops/online_field/activities/236670.html
  The page says the Leeds Lighthouse Bay landscape is available under
  CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and that the materials posted there are shared under the
  same license.

- Mark Helper, The University of Texas at Austin. Geologic Mapping of a
  Virtual Landscape II — Three River Hills, based on the Leeds landscape.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/NAGTWorkshops/online_field/activities/237061.html

Houghton, Lloyd, Robinson, Gordon, and Morgan (2015), The Virtual Worlds
Project: Geological mapping and field skills, Geology Today, 31, 227–231.
https://doi.org/10.1111/gto.12117

Visible Geology SERC activity pages
-----------------------------------

Collection index, fetched:
https://serc.carleton.edu/visiblegeology/teaching_materials/index.html

Each activity page below was fetched. The worksheet files on those pages are
stated as CC BY-NC-SA 3.0.

Exercises 1–3 (topographic maps, dip, and the Rule of V's). Course text credits
Alyssa Abbey, California State University, Long Beach.

- How geologic beds and other planar features interact with topography:
  Rule of V's. Alyssa Abbey.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/visiblegeology/teaching_materials/310972.html

Exercise 6 (stereonets and fold map patterns). Course text credits Hannah
Blatchford, Eastern Michigan University.

- Exploring relationships between map patterns and stereonets of cylindrically
  folded layers. Hannah Blatchford.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/visiblegeology/teaching_materials/311066.html

Exercise 8 (basin stratigraphic correlation). Course text credits Amy
Weislogel, West Virginia University.

- Basin Stratigraphic Correlation. Amy Weislogel.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/visiblegeology/teaching_materials/311247.html

Exercise 9 (reservoir sandstone and CO2 storage). Course text credits Amy
Weislogel.

- Reservoir Sandstone Stratigraphic Correlation. Amy Weislogel.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/visiblegeology/teaching_materials/311249.html

Exercise 10 (map and cross section built into a 3D model). The closest fetched
SERC activity is Blatchford's page below. That activity is written for any
previously mapped field area (the SERC notes use a subset of the Argenta,
Montana quadrangle). This course asks students to model Rhoscolyn.

- Combining Map and Cross Section Results into a 3-D Model. Hannah Blatchford.
  https://serc.carleton.edu/visiblegeology/teaching_materials/311065.html

